# Which application release runs on which PHP version, and how.
#
# One row per fixture set (an application release, populated once), with the
# explicit list of PHP versions it is tested on. Rows may overlap and may leave
# gaps: two releases of one app can run on the same PHP, and a PHP version
# nobody asked for simply has no cell. There is no coverage rule; the cells are
# exactly what is written here. `appsets.py check` (run by run-matrix.sh)
# validates the syntax, that every PHP version exists in matrix.json, that
# every (app, set) has a fixture recipe and apps.lock rows, and that no
# (app, set) or PHP version is listed twice.
#
# php    comma list of matrix.json versions; a-b is shorthand for every
#        matrix.json version from a to b (7.3-8.0 is 7.3,7.4,8.0)
# flags  comma list, or - for none:
#          builder   also run the cli-builder image (composer against the real
#                    lock); without it a row runs fpm and cli only
#          v3        also run the -v3 images, where the PHP version has one
#                    (matrix.json "uarch"); meant for the latest release of an app
#          hardened  fpm also runs the hardened (snuffleupagus) config, on the
#                    PHP versions that ship it (php/ext.json: >= 7.2)
# db     the database the fixture is installed on; only mariadb is supported.
#        A column per row because one fixture is one database.
#
# ext-builder images never run application suites; they have their own test.
#
# The PHP the fixture is BUILT on is the lowest version in the row: every set
# is installed, composer-resolved and populated on the stock image for it
# (APPTEST_BUILDER_REPO, dementev/php-fpm-with-ext by default), so the data the
# from-source images are tested against was written by a known-good PHP they
# have to stay compatible with -- serialized sessions, password hashes, cache
# entries and all. The recipe hash carries only that lowest version, so adding
# or dropping a PHP the row is tested on never rebuilds its fixture.
#
# app        set    php        flags                 db
laravel      9      8.0,8.2    hardened              mariadb
laravel      10     8.1,8.3    hardened              mariadb
laravel      12     8.2,8.5    hardened              mariadb
laravel      13     8.3-8.5    builder,v3,hardened   mariadb
# No hardened on 4.9 and 5.9: their sets are built on 7.0, so they carry that era's
# code, which require_once's non-.php files the rulesets drop by design --
# Twenty Seventeen's svg-icons.svg (4.9) and the symfony/polyfill-mbstring < 1.19
# bundled in WPForms Lite 1.9.2.3, whose .php8 shim loads on PHP 8 (5.9 on 8.1).
wordpress    4.9    7.0,7.2    -                     mariadb
wordpress    5.9    7.0,8.1    -                     mariadb
wordpress    6.9    7.2,8.5    hardened              mariadb
wordpress    7.1    7.4-8.5    builder,v3,hardened   mariadb
# PrestaShop. The PHP lists follow what was measured to work, not each release's
# documented ceiling (PrestaShop's own devdocs stop 8.2 at 8.1): 8.2.8 is tested
# up to 8.4 with its cache:warmup allowed to fail there (tests/apps/known-failures: its Twig 3.4.3
# compiles the closure-based format_date function into invalid PHP under 8.4's
# closure names); 1.7.8.11 stops at 7.3 because its webservice error handler
# turns 7.4's "array offset on null/bool" notices into HTTP 500s
# (WebserviceOutputBuilder.php:669, Product.php:7178).
# 1.6 is the only PrestaShop that runs on 7.0; builder only on 8.2 and 9.2 (the
# lines whose lock composer replays), v3 only on the newest line (9.2).
prestashop   1.6    7.0-7.1        hardened              mariadb
prestashop   1.7    7.1-7.3        hardened              mariadb
prestashop   8.0    7.4,8.1        hardened              mariadb
prestashop   8.2    7.2,8.1-8.4    builder,hardened      mariadb
prestashop   9.2    8.1-8.5        builder,v3,hardened   mariadb
